    batch: 1,
    title: "2026年9月，江苏昆山举办戏曲百戏盛典，46个剧种集中展演，戏曲街头快闪覆盖园林、商圈等公共空间（注：该事件同时涉及社会与文化交叉领域，此处从“公共文化服务融入日常生活”的社会视角切入）。",
    units: 332,
    text: "In September 2026, the city of Kunshan in Jiangsu Province hosted a grand gathering of traditional Chinese opera. Forty-six different opera forms from across the country took part in the event, with performances held in theaters, parks and public squares over several days.\n\nWhat made this event different was not just the number of opera forms on stage, but where the performances took place. Organizers arranged more than 300 “opera flash mobs” throughout the year in gardens, shopping districts, museums and other public spaces. A short piece from a traditional opera might suddenly begin in a classical garden, drawing passersby who had never bought a ticket to an opera performance before. In one case, a group of young performers staged a brief scene in a busy commercial street, and within minutes a crowd of shoppers had stopped to watch.\n\nLocal authorities say the goal is to bring opera out of dedicated theaters and into everyday life. “We want people to encounter opera while they are walking their dog, shopping for groceries or visiting a museum,” one organizer explained. “You do not need to understand every word to feel something.”\n\nAlongside the street performances, the city has developed opera-themed tourism routes, cultural products and markets featuring traditional crafts. Visitors can follow a map that guides them to different performance locations, taste local snacks and buy handmade items related to the operas they have just seen. The approach has attracted younger audiences in particular. According to local reports, ticket sales for full-length opera performances have also risen, suggesting that brief street encounters are encouraging people to seek out more.\n\nThe event reflects a broader shift in how traditional culture is being shared with the public in China. Rather than treating heritage as something to be preserved behind glass, cities are experimenting with ways to weave it into daily routines, commerce and tourism. The results so far suggest that when tradition meets people where they already are, it can find new audiences and new energy.",
    reference: "2026年9月，江苏省昆山市举办了一场传统戏曲盛会。来自全国各地的46个剧种参加了此次活动，连续数天在剧场、公园和公共广场演出。\n\n这次活动的不同之处不仅在于登台的剧种数量，更在于演出的地点。主办方全年在园林、商圈、博物馆和其他公共空间安排了300多场“戏曲快闪”。一段传统戏曲可能突然在古典园林中响起，吸引从未买票看过戏的路人驻足。有一次，一群年轻演员在繁忙的商业街上表演了一个短片段，几分钟内就有大批购物者停下来观看。\n\n当地有关部门表示，目标是把戏曲从专门的剧场带到日常生活中去。“我们希望人们在遛狗、买菜或逛博物馆时就能遇到戏曲，”一位组织者解释说，“你不需要听懂每一个字，也能感受到一些东西。”\n\n除了街头演出，这座城市还开发了戏曲主题旅游线路、文创产品和非遗市集。游客可以按照地图指引前往不同的演出地点，品尝当地小吃，购买与刚看过的戏曲相关的手工艺品。这种方式尤其吸引了年轻观众。据当地报道，整本大戏的票房也有所上升，说明街头短暂相遇正在鼓励人们进一步了解戏曲。\n\n这一活动反映了中国传统文化面向公众传播方式的更广泛转变。城市不再把遗产当作放在玻璃后面保存的东西，而是在尝试将其融入日常生活、商业和旅游。迄今为止的结果表明，当传统在人们已有的生活场景中出现时，它能够找到新的观众和新的活力。",
