    title: "2026年9月，北京朝阳区太阳宫乡西坝河北里社区4号楼通过楼门“微治理”推动全龄友好，居民自发成立自治管家队和环境监督队。",
    units: 361,
    text: "In September 2026, a community in Beijing’s Chaoyang District completed a renovation project that turned an aging residential building into a model of what local authorities call “all-age friendly” living. The building, located in the Xibahe Beili community, had long suffered from peeling walls, cluttered hallways and weak maintenance. It was home to a mix of elderly residents living alone, families with young children and people with disabilities.\n\nRather than hiring outside contractors and asking residents to stay out of the way, the community took a different approach. Residents of all ages were invited to participate in the renovation. Elderly residents helped clear clutter and organize shared spaces. Middle-aged residents took on wall repairs and painting. Children decorated a cultural wall with drawings and paintings. The result was a corridor that residents felt they had built together.\n\nThe design also took different needs into account. The cultural wall, decorated by children, gave young residents a sense of ownership. Public notice boards were printed in large fonts with high contrast to make them easier for elderly residents to read. Residents donated plants and worked together to turn an unused corner outside the building into a small shared garden.\n\nAfter the renovation, residents set up two volunteer teams on their own initiative. One team handles routine maintenance of the corridor and walls. The other conducts regular inspections to make sure fire exits are clear and to address any safety issues. This has shifted the building from a place managed by the community office to one managed by the people who live there.\n\nThe community also uses traditional festivals as opportunities to bring residents together. During the Dragon Boat Festival, neighbors gathered to make zongzi. Volunteers then delivered the rice dumplings they had made to elderly residents living alone. At a Mid-Autumn Festival event, seniors, young adults and children made mooncakes together, with older residents passing on techniques and children enjoying the experience.\n\nOne longtime elderly resident said the changes are visible and tangible. “The corridor is clean and bright, the walls are covered with the children’s artwork, and there is greenery right outside the door,” she said. “It feels warm to live here.”",
    reference: "2026年9月，北京市朝阳区一个社区完成了一项改造工程，把一栋老旧居民楼变成了当地所说的“全龄友好”生活的样板。这栋楼位于西坝河北里社区，长期以来墙面斑驳、楼道堆物、管护薄弱。楼内住着独居老人、有幼儿的家庭和残障居民等不同群体。\n\n社区没有聘请外部施工队、让居民回避，而是采取了不同的做法。社区邀请各年龄段居民共同参与改造。老年居民帮忙清理杂物、整理公共空间；中年居民负责修补和粉刷墙面；孩子们用绘画装饰了一面文化墙。最终，居民们觉得这条走廊是自己亲手建起来的。\n\n设计上也考虑到了不同需求。孩子们装饰的文化墙让年轻居民有了参与家园建设的归属感。公共提示版面采用大字体、高对比度设计，方便老年人阅读。居民自发捐出绿植，合力把楼前闲置角落打造成一个温馨的小微共享花园。\n\n改造完成后，居民自发成立了两支志愿队伍。一支负责楼道和墙面的日常维护；另一支定期巡查，确保消防通道畅通，及时处理安全隐患。这使这栋楼从由社区办公室管理转变为由居民自己管理。\n\n社区还以传统节日为契机，把居民聚在一起。端午节期间，邻居们一起包粽子，志愿者随后把亲手包的粽子送到独居老人家中。中秋节活动中，老人、年轻人和孩子一起做月饼，长辈传授手艺，孩子们享受体验。\n\n一位居住多年的老年居民说，变化看得见、摸得着。“楼道干净敞亮，墙上都是孩子们的作品，出门就能看到绿色，”她说，“住着舒心、心里暖和。”",
