    units: 343,
    text: "Just before New Year’s Day in 2026, a new community center opened in a residential area of Beijing’s Tongzhou District. The building had once been an old auditorium that had fallen into disrepair and was declared unsafe. Rather than demolish it, the local government renovated it into a multi-purpose space serving residents of all ages.\n\nThe center now houses a supermarket, a canteen, a barber shop, a health consultation room, a children’s after-school care area, a sports training space and a tea corner with a reading nook. It is designed to serve about 3,000 households within a five-minute walk. The renovation kept the red-brick texture of the original auditorium. The north building was rebuilt largely in its original form, while the south building uses a modular design with larger windows and better natural light.\n\nWhat makes this center notable is not just its facilities but how it was planned. Before construction began, the project team spent six months holding four rounds of meetings with resident representatives, conducting door-to-door visits and displaying design proposals for public comment. Dozens of suggestions were collected. When some residents raised concerns about lighting, staff visited their homes with design renderings to explain the plans in person and ease their worries.\n\nA similar center in the same district, converted from an old boiler room, has been in use for more than four years. The red-brick facade and the “safety-qualified boiler room” sign beside the entrance were deliberately preserved. Inside, a 14-meter-high atrium where boilers once stood now has skylights that let sunlight reach the ground floor. Residents come here to order meals, read, exercise or visit the community health station.\n\nThe plan is to build 36 such centers across the district, each serving as a kind of city-level property management hub that fills gaps in public services. Local officials say the key principle is that residents decide what goes into the space and how it is used. “This is not the government making decisions from the top down,” one official explained. “It is about respecting what people actually want.”",
    reference: "2026年元旦前夕，北京市通州区一个居民小区里，一座新的社区中心投入使用。这栋建筑曾经是一座年久失修、被鉴定为危房的老旧礼堂。当地政府没有将其拆除，而是改造成了一座服务全年龄段居民的多功能空间。\n\n如今，这座中心里有超市、食堂、理发店、健康问诊室、少儿托管区、运动培训空间，还有一个带读书角的养生茶饮店。它的设计服务半径是步行五分钟，可辐射周边约3000户居民。改造保留了老礼堂的红砖建筑肌理，北侧建筑基本按原貌复建，南侧建筑采用模块化形式，窗户更大，采光更好。\n\n这座中心引人注目的不仅是设施，还有它的规划方式。开工前，项目团队历时半年，先后组织了四轮居民代表座谈会，进行入户走访，并公示设计方案征求意见，收集到数十条建议。当有居民对采光问题提出担忧时，工作人员带着设计效果图上门详细讲解，打消了他们的疑虑。\n\n同区另一座由老旧锅炉房改造而成的类似中心，已经投用四年多。红砖外立面和大门一侧的“安全合格锅炉房”标识牌被特意保留下来。内部，过去放锅炉的地方挑高14米，现在屋顶开了天窗，阳光可以直接照到大堂地面。居民来这里订餐、读书、锻炼，或者到社区卫生服务站看病拿药。\n\n按照规划，该区将建设36座这样的中心，每座相当于一个城市级物业管理枢纽，用来补齐公共服务设施的短板。当地官员表示，核心原则是让居民决定空间里放什么、怎么用。“这不是政府自上而下的大包大揽，”一位官员解释说，“而是充分尊重老百姓真正需要什么。”",
