    units: 385,
    text: "In January 2026, a subdistrict in Beijing’s Haidian District upgraded its meal delivery program for elderly residents into a round-the-clock service covering breakfast, lunch and dinner. The program, originally launched to solve the “lunch problem” for elderly people living alone, now covers all 22 communities in the subdistrict.\n\nThe program began several years ago when local officials noticed that many elderly residents who lived alone or whose children worked during the day had trouble preparing hot meals. Volunteers started delivering lunch to these residents. Over time, the program expanded. Now, elderly residents can call a hotline to order all three meals. Lunch and dinner offer a set meal of “one meat dish, two vegetable dishes and a staple,” priced at just 18 yuan. Breakfast is delivered free of charge on weekends by volunteers to those over 80 who live alone.\n\nThe meals are prepared by local restaurants that have partnered with the subdistrict. The dishes are designed to be soft, low in salt and oil, and easy to chew. According to a staff member, the menu changes daily and includes options for residents with diabetes or high blood pressure. “We want the food to be both nutritious and something they actually enjoy eating,” the staff member said.\n\nWhat makes this program notable is not just the food but the network behind it. The subdistrict has mobilized community workers, volunteers and local restaurants to work together. Some volunteers are neighbors who live in the same building. Others are college students who sign up for weekend shifts. For elderly residents who have difficulty using a phone, community workers visit regularly to take their orders in person.\n\nAn 85-year-old resident who has used the service for over a year said it has changed her daily life. “I used to skip lunch because it was too much trouble to cook for one person,” she said. “Now I get a hot meal every day, and someone comes to the door. It is not just the food — it is knowing that someone is thinking of you.”\n\nThe subdistrict plans to expand the program further, adding more meal options and extending delivery to residents under 80 who have mobility issues. Officials say the goal is to build a system where no elderly resident has to worry about whether they will eat well.",
    reference: "2026年1月，北京市海淀区某街道将其为老年居民提供的送餐项目升级为覆盖早餐、午餐和晚餐的全天候服务。该项目最初是为了解决独居老人的“午餐难”问题而启动的，如今已覆盖该街道全部22个社区。\n\n该项目始于几年前，当时当地工作人员注意到，许多独居或子女白天上班的老年居民做饭有困难。志愿者开始为这些居民送午餐。随着时间推移，项目不断扩大。现在，老年居民可以拨打热线预订三餐。午、晚餐提供“一荤两素一主食”的套餐，仅需18元。周末，志愿者为80岁以上独居老人免费配送早餐。\n\n餐食由与街道合作的社会餐饮企业制作。菜品设计得软烂、低盐低油、易于咀嚼。据一位工作人员介绍，菜单每日更换，并为患有糖尿病或高血压的居民提供选择。“我们希望饭菜既有营养，又是他们真正喜欢吃的，”这位工作人员说。\n\n这个项目引人注目的不仅是餐食本身，还有其背后的服务网络。街道动员了社区工作人员、志愿者和辖区餐饮企业共同参与。有些志愿者是住在同一栋楼里的邻居，还有些是报名参加周末班次的大学生。对于使用电话有困难的老年居民，社区工作人员会定期上门当面登记订餐需求。\n\n一位85岁的居民使用这项服务已有一年多，她说这改变了她的日常生活。“以前我一个人懒得做饭，经常不吃午饭，”她说，“现在我每天都能吃上热乎饭，还有人送到门口。这不仅仅是饭菜的问题——是知道有人在惦记着你。”\n\n该街道计划进一步扩大项目，增加更多餐食选择，并将配送范围扩展到80岁以下但有行动困难的居民。工作人员表示，目标是建立一个让每一位老年居民都不必担心吃不好饭的服务体系。",
